  <Fragment slot="description">
    <p>El Mar Hirviendo is a fifteen year observation of the surface of the sea, made from the same pier in Barcelona. I photograph and paint the water only from above, looking down without a horizon, only the surface.</p>
    <p>I am afraid of deep water and for years I have dreamed of waves coming toward me. Observing the surface, imagining what lies beneath, photographing and painting it, is a way of staying present with something I cannot otherwise approach. This practice became a ritual. I stand at the same place, in different seasons and light, watching the water do its work. Over the years the photographs accumulated into an archive of hundreds of images of the same surface, where repetition reveals what a single visit never could.</p>
    <p>For most of human history the sea was understood not as a landscape but as a living presence, a threshold to the world beneath the visible, kept in relation through ritual rather than looked at as a view. That older understanding is close to what this work is doing. The surface is where something present and active becomes visible without ever fully showing itself, and the practice is a way of staying in contact with what is underneath without pretending it can be seen or known.</p>
    <p>To paint water without repeating its clichés I divide each photograph into a grid and paint each square as its own abstraction. The method gives me a way into something that resists being held all at once. Past a certain point, the method becomes the thing I have to escape, and I step back and ask whether the painting feels like the water rather than looks like it.</p>
    <p>The title, the boiling sea, comes from how the surface looks photographed from above without a horizon. It appears to boil from within, agitated by forces invisible from above. The sea that looks calm from a distance is always moving underneath.</p>
  </Fragment>
